{
  "example": "The model calls `list_files` to find a directory, then `ripgrep` to find matching\nlines in a Rust file. The listing creates no evidence input; the search returns\nand records file-range inputs including five context lines on each side, where\navailable. A `habeas` request with that file's `rust_file` and\na `rust_item` selector returns and records the complete Rust item, along with any\nrequired terminology. A file-range request omitting `line_end` reads through EOF\nand records the actual ending line without requiring the model to know it.\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.file_discovery`\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.text_search`\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.source_tools`\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.delivered_inputs`",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests cover each step of the `additional_read` scenario, and the example is referenced by `{test}` in three places. I judged this from the supplied test code only and did not read the `dispatch` implementation.\n\n**Unit test `reading_records_only_delivered_sources_and_their_terms`** in `src/authoring/tools.rs` carries `{test}` for `additional_read`.\n- `list_files` on `src` returns `[{\"path\":\"src/lib.rs\",\"kind\":\"file\"}]` and `listing.inputs.is_empty()`. This covers \"the listing creates no evidence input\".\n- `habeas` with `rust_file` and `rust_item` (`fn[work]`) returns 2 inputs: the Rust item and the `terms.value` definition. The result's `definitions[0].definition` is `terms.value`. This covers returning the item with its required terminology.\n- `recorded_inputs()` stays empty until `record_inputs` is called. After two calls with the same inputs it holds 2, so recording is deduplicated.\n- A `habeas` file request with `line_start: 2` and no `line_end` returns `line_end == 2` and the text `[\"fn work() {}\"]`. This covers \"reads through EOF and records the actual ending line\".\n- `line_start: 0` and a range of malformed, mixed and unknown request forms are rejected. The requirements-only and definition-only requests succeed.\n\n**Unit test `search_merges_context_windows_and_respects_ignore_rules`** also carries `{test}` for `additional_read`.\n- Matches on lines 2 and 6 give one merged file-range input covering lines 1–11 with the exact text. That is five context lines on each side, clamped at the start of the file.\n- `context: 0` gives only the matching lines, as 2 separate inputs.\n- Ignored and hidden files are excluded, a case-mismatched pattern returns empty arrays, and an invalid pattern or negative context returns an error.\n- `list_files` with `../` returns an error.\n\n**CLI test `ollama_cli_delivers_sources_records_fixme_and_keeps_usage_content_free`** in `tests/authoring_cli.rs` carries `{test}` for `additional_read`. It drives the whole sequence through the model loop:\n- `list_files`, then `ripgrep` on `extra.txt`, then `habeas` file reads, including a repeated read and a failing read of `absent.txt`.\n- The written evidence contains the `extra.txt` ranges 1–11 (from search) and 10–12 (from the EOF read), deduplicated to 2 inputs.\n- The evidence does not contain the directory listing (`file == \".\"`) or the failed `absent.txt` read, so listings and failed reads create no inputs.\n\nTogether these cover the example's scenario and its expected outcomes. The tests do not assert that the Rust-item text is the whole item, but they do check that a Rust-item input is delivered and recorded with its terminology. I found no acknowledged FIXME gaps.",
